<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1633">1633</a>–<a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Anton Ulrich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Ulrich was Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, co-ruling with his brother from 1685 and alone from 1704. A rival of the Hanoverian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a>, he was also a novelist, author of long courtly romances, and an art collector whose collections later formed the Herzog Anton Ulrich Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. He arranged his granddaughter's marriage to the future Emperor Charles VI and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1710.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-ulrich-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Bernhard Francke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Herzog_Anton_Ulrich_von_Braunschweig_(1633-1714).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anthony Ulrich was born on 4 October 1633 at Hitzacker, the second surviving son of Duke Augustus the Younger of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and Princess Dorothea of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>; the next year his father took over the Principality of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. He was educated at court by scholars such as Justus Georg Schottel and Sigmund von Birken, took a doctorate in theology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-helmstedt">University of Helmstedt</a>, and on his Grand Tour met Madeleine de Scudéry. In 1656 he married his cousin Elizabeth Juliana of Schleswig-Holstein-Sønderburg-Norburg; they had 13 children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father died in 1666, his elder brother Rudolph Augustus, who preferred hunting and his library to government, left most decisions to him. He brought the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> to accept ducal overlordship in 1671. When his cousin Ernest Augustus received the new ninth electorship in 1692, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> brothers felt cheated of it, and in the War of the Spanish Succession Anthony Ulrich sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Hanover and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> invaded in March 1702 and he was deposed by the emperor's order and fled to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>.</p><p>He returned to power on his brother's death in 1704 and reached a settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1706. He then approached the Imperial Habsburg dynasty: his granddaughter Elisabeth Christine married Archduke Charles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and he lived to see Charles elected Emperor Charles VI in 1711. When he himself converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> he promised his subjects it would not affect his government. He died on 27 March 1714 at Schloss Salzdahlum and was succeeded by his son Augustus William.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>An admirer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and a proponent of enlightened absolutism, he brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a> to court and built a public opera house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> in 1689 and 1690. He enlarged his father's Bibliotheca Augusta, hired the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> as librarian and supported Anton Wilhelm Amo. He completed his sister Sibylle Ursula's courtly novel Aramena and wrote Die Römische Octavia, and his collection became the foundation of the Herzog Anton Ulrich Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Anthony Ulrich</b> gave it to his younger son Louis Rudolph, and in 1707 Emperor Joseph I raised it to a principality, giving Louis Rudolph a seat among the princes of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-blankenburg">Principality of Blankenburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hitzacker, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Salzdahlum, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.15,11.04&amp;year=1685" aria-label="Hitzacker on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 334 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anthony Ulrich, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.15,11.04&amp;year=1685">What was here in 1685? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
